# Bundles are made on top of origin, never beside it

Work moves between the office machine and the home machine by bundle, and the
home machine pushes to `origin`. So the local branch is usually behind by the
time a bundle is asked for, and a bundle built on a stale base will not apply.

Before creating any bundle, in this order:

1. `git fetch origin` and compare with `git status -sb`.
2. If the branch is behind, `git pull --rebase origin main`. Do this even when
   the user only asked for a bundle; it is part of making one.
3. Build the range from the remote, never from a remembered commit:
   `git bundle create <path> origin/main..main`.
4. `git bundle verify <path>` and state which commit the bundle requires, so the
   user can tell whether their other machine has it.

## After a rebase, check what upstream did to the links

Copying a fresh `build/sessions/` over `content/G5eC/log/` throws away every
wikilink, and the commit that does it reads as ordinary text edits. It has
happened twice. After rebasing, run `python tools/autolink/autolink.py`; if the
preview wants to write links that were already there, upstream dropped them, so
say so and re-apply before bundling.

## Housekeeping

Delete the previous bundle file only once its commits are on `origin`. If the
user says an older bundle was already applied, that is the signal to fetch,
because `origin` has almost certainly moved.
